---
/**
 * The four plans, side by side, and the one input that says which of them is cheapest for you.
 *
 * ── Why a comparison and not a feature matrix ──
 * Every tier includes exactly the same product. The ONLY thing that differs is the ratio between
 * the monthly fee and the per-sale commission (`lib/pricing.ts` — the ladder is tuned so each tier
 * owns a real revenue band). A table with ticks and crosses would therefore have to invent
 * differences, and inventing one is inventing a product. So the page states the sameness plainly and
 * spends its space on the only question a seller actually has: which row is cheapest for me.
 *
 * ── The numbers are read, never typed ──
 * `SELLER_TIERS` is the single source since 2026-07-27, and every figure on this page interpolates
 * from it. `+ מע״מ` sits beside each one because these are B2B prices the seller reclaims — the
 * convention is the opposite of the storefront's, and pricing.ts's header is where that is argued.
 *
 * ── The calculator's arithmetic lives in the DOM, not in a JSON blob ──
 * Each card carries its own fee and commission as `data-` attributes, so the script re-reads the
 * same numbers the page rendered rather than a second copy of them shipped alongside. It also keeps
 * a `<script is:inline>`-shaped JSON payload off the page entirely (memory
 * `project_json_script_xss`).
 *
 * ── The page ARRIVES with an answer on it (2026-08-24) ──
 * Owner: *"הדף מאוד מת"*. It was: four cards with an empty line where the figure goes, and nothing
 * on screen until somebody typed. So the example below is computed HERE, on the server, and the
 * field opens holding it — the reader sees a worked comparison at a glance, and the same script
 * recomputes the moment he changes the number. It also means the whole calculator works with
 * JavaScript off, which the empty version did not.
 *
 * **5,000₪ is the example on purpose.** It is a plausible first year, and it is the band where the
 * honest answer is "these plans are within twenty-odd shekels of each other" — which is what the
 * page says there, rather than dressing a 24₪ gap as a saving (owner: *"המחשבון מגוחך"*).
 */
import { SELLER_TIERS } from '../lib/pricing.js';
import type { getT } from '../i18n/index.js';
import type { SellerTierId } from '../lib/pricing.js';

interface Props {
  p: ReturnType<typeof getT>['pricing'];
  lang: 'he' | 'en';
  /** Only a signed-in seller is told where his choice landed — for a visitor the same click is a
   *  redirect to registration, and a line naming a dashboard tab he has no account for is noise. */
  isLoggedIn: boolean;
}
const { p, lang, isLoggedIn } = Astro.props;

/** The worked example the page opens on. Not a placeholder digit — every figure below is this
 *  number run through the same arithmetic the script uses. */
const EXAMPLE_REVENUE = 5000;
const locale = lang === 'he' ? 'he-IL' : 'en-GB';
const ils = (n: number): string => `₪${Math.round(n).toLocaleString(locale)}`;

const costs = SELLER_TIERS.map((tier) => ({
  tier,
  cost: tier.monthlyFee + (EXAMPLE_REVENUE * tier.commissionPercent) / 100,
}));
const cheapest = costs.reduce((a, b) => (b.cost < a.cost ? b : a));
const dearest = costs.reduce((a, b) => (b.cost > a.cost ? b : a));
const spread = dearest.cost - cheapest.cost;
/** "Near enough identical" is relative to the BILL, not an absolute shekel figure: 24₪ off 699₪ is
 *  noise, 24₪ off 220₪ is not. Five percent is the line, and `scripts/pricing-tiers.ts` holds the
 *  same one — it is the same sentence, recomputed. */
const isClose = spread < cheapest.cost * 0.05;
const summary = isClose
  ? p.calcClose.replace('{amount}', ils(spread))
  : p.calcGap
      .replace('{tier}', p.tiers[cheapest.tier.id as SellerTierId].name)
      .replace('{amount}', ils(spread))
      .replace('{other}', p.tiers[dearest.tier.id as SellerTierId].name);
---

<section class="mt-8" aria-labelledby="calc-title">
  <h2 id="calc-title" class="text-[1.05rem] font-bold m-0">{p.calcTitle}</h2>
  <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.88rem] max-w-[46rem]">{p.sameEverything}</p>
  <div class="pricing-panel mt-3">
    <label for="calc-revenue" class="block font-semibold text-[0.92rem]">{p.calcQuestion}</label>
    {/* `inputmode="numeric"` and not `type="number"`: the field takes a formatted amount with
        separators, and a number input rejects them outright on some browsers and silently blanks
        itself on others. The script parses digits out of whatever is typed. */}
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mt-2">
      <span class="text-[1.05rem] font-bold" aria-hidden="true">₪</span>
      <input
        id="calc-revenue"
        type="text"
        inputmode="numeric"
        autocomplete="off"
        value={EXAMPLE_REVENUE.toLocaleString(locale)}
        placeholder={p.calcPlaceholder}
        class="input max-w-[12rem]"
      />
    </div>
    {/* The sentence the whole calculator exists to produce, and the only line under the field —
        the hint that used to sit beneath it ("enter an amount and we will mark the cheapest") said
        what this sentence demonstrates. `aria-live` because it changes under a field being typed
        in. */}
    <p class="m-0 mt-3 text-[0.9rem] font-semibold" id="calc-summary" aria-live="polite">{summary}</p>
  </div>
</section>

<ul
  id="tier-list"
  role="list"
  class="list-none p-0 m-0 mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4"
  data-label-best={p.calcBest}
  data-label-close={p.calcClose}
  data-label-gap={p.calcGap}
  data-label-choose={p.choose}
  data-label-current={p.current}
  data-label-saving={p.saving}
  data-label-saved={p.saved}
  data-label-failed={p.saveFailed}
>
  {costs.map(({ tier }) => {
    const copy = p.tiers[tier.id as SellerTierId];
    const best = tier.id === cheapest.tier.id;
    // `card--flat`: the card itself is not clickable — the button inside it is — and a shadow on
    // hover would claim otherwise (Hard rules → a shadow means the surface FLOATS).
    // The calculator sets `data-best` on the winning card; what that LOOKS like is decided in
    // `cards.css`, beside the `.card` rule it has to beat — a Tailwind
    // `data-[best]:[border-color:…]` here would be dead, because unlayered legacy CSS wins over a
    // layered utility whatever the specificity. It was written that way first and
    // `tests/unlayered-css-beats-utility.test.ts` refused it.
    return (
      <li
        class="card card--flat p-4 flex flex-col"
        data-tier={tier.id}
        data-fee={String(tier.monthlyFee)}
        data-commission={String(tier.commissionPercent)}
        data-best={best ? '' : undefined}
      >
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2">
          <h3 class="text-[1.05rem] font-bold m-0">{copy.name}</h3>
          {/* Rendered on every card and shown on one — the winner moves as the field changes, and a
              badge the script has to build is a badge that arrives a frame late. */}
          <span class="pricing-best-badge" data-role="best-badge">{p.calcBest}</span>
        </div>
        {/* The audience line ("for a shop just starting out") is gone: the calculator answers the
            same question with the seller's own number, and four cards each carrying six facts is
            what made the page read as complicated. */}
        <p class="m-0 mt-3 flex items-baseline gap-1">
          <span class="text-[1.45rem] font-bold tabular-nums">₪{tier.monthlyFee}</span>
          <span class="muted text-[0.84rem]">{p.perMonth}</span>
        </p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-1 text-[0.88rem]">
          <span class="font-semibold tabular-nums">{tier.commissionPercent}%</span>
          <span class="muted"> {p.commissionLabel}</span>
        </p>

        {/* **The monthly bill is deliberately NOT on this card** (owner, 2026-08-24: *"לא צריך
            להראות למוכר כמה הוא ישלם לנו בחודש עם המכירות, זה רעיון גרוע, זה נראה מפחיד... בראש שלו
            זה נראה כאילו למה לי לשלם על חנות כל כך הרבה כסף בחודש? בלי שהוא מבין שזה כולל את כל
            הסליקה והכל"*).
            It was here for a day, as "₪675 לחודש · 13.5% מהמחזור", and the reasoning was that a
            figure with no size means nothing. That is true of a PRICE and wrong about this number:
            fee-plus-commission at a revenue he just guessed is not a price, it is a bill he has not
            earned yet, and it lands beside a 99₪ subscription as if the subscription had been a
            lie. **And it was not even the real total** — PayMe take a clearing fee on top of both
            numbers (`docs/payme-correspondence.md`), so what the page called "סה״כ לחודש" was a
            figure nobody would ever be charged (owner, 2026-08-24: *"אין פה שיקוף אמיתי של המחיר אז
            אין בשביל מה להראות אותו"*).
            **The saving survives that objection and the total does not**, which is why one stayed:
            the clearing fee is the same on every plan, so it cancels in a difference between two of
            them. "Growth saves you 500₪ a month" is exact; "you will pay 675₪" never was. What the calculator says instead is which plan is cheapest FOR HIM, and — only when
            the gap is worth acting on — what choosing it saves. */}

        {/* Pushed to the bottom so the four buttons line up however uneven the copy above them is.
            The cheapest card's button is the filled one: the page has four identical actions and
            exactly one of them is the answer to the question the reader just asked. */}
        <button
          type="button"
          class:list={['btn btn--sm mt-4', best ? 'btn--accent' : 'btn--ghost']}
          data-role="choose"
        >
          {p.choose}
        </button>
      </li>
    );
  })}
</ul>

{/* Two facts on one line under the prices, where the two sections they used to have would have
    been: when we start charging, and that these are pre-VAT figures. */}
<p class="muted m-0 mt-3 text-[0.82rem]">{p.whenBody} {p.vatNote}</p>

{isLoggedIn && (
  <p class="!hidden m-0 mt-3 text-[0.88rem]" id="tier-saved-where">
    <span>{p.savedWhere}</span>{' '}
    <a href="/seller/dashboard?panel=payouts">{p.savedWhereLink}</a>
  </p>
)}
